          "h1": "Andrew Chincea",
          "noscript": [],
          "content": "Andrew Chincea\n\nWhat can the shape of a landscape tell us about the water that moves through it?\n\nwater quality\ntopological data analysis\nstatistics\nnumber theory\nAnimation\nRising water\nDye spill\nFractal pond\nRandom rivers\nCollatz delta\nSettings\nPlay\nNew terrain\n\n \n\nWhen the animation has focus, Space plays or pauses. The Left and Right arrows lower and raise the water, and Home and End jump to the bottom and the top. Up and Down pick a bar, Escape clears it, and N makes new terrain.\n\nWater rises over a random landscape. Lakes form in the valleys, and when two meet, the older one carries on. The barcode gives each lake a bar from the level where it forms to the level where it merges. See it on the Colorado River Basin →\nMission\n\nI study mathematics (statistics concentration) and data science and analytics at Kennesaw State University. My main research is on RiverNets, a project in the KSU TDA Lab, where I use graph-Laplacian regression to predict water quality at river stations that have no data. I also work in analytic number theory; as a First-Year Scholar, I studied eigenvalues of the fractal Laplacian.\n\nRecent work\n\nPAPERTsz Ho Chan and Andrew Chincea. Largest kth-Power Divisor of a Random Integer. Submitted.\n\nPOSTERAndrew Chincea and Yunus Kaya (Dec 2026). RiverNets: Graph-Laplacian Regression on River Networks for Water Quality Prediction at Unmonitored Stations in the Colorado River Basin. KSU Fall Analytics Day, Kennesaw State University, Kennesaw, GA.\n\nPROJECTAndrew Chincea (Nov 2026 – Present). OwlPath.\n\nPROJECTAndrew Chincea (Nov 2026). Personal portfolio.\n\nPROJECTAndrew Chincea (Nov 2026). Rising water.\n\nAll work →\n\nResearch\nIllustration\nWater-quality predicti",
