          "content": "RISING WATER\n\nColorado River Basin study area (RiverNets coverage)\n\nWater rises over the study area. Lakes form in the valleys. When two meet, the older one carries on. Each bar runs from the water level where its lake forms to the level where it merges. The lowest lake’s bar never ends. The water on the map and the barcode are computed in your browser from the same 326 × 414 elevation grid; the shaded relief under the water is precomputed.\n\nPoint at or tap the map to read a cell’s elevation and coordinates.\n\nThe lowest lake and the 11 longest-lived lakes each have their own colour and a label at their pit (“lowest”, 1–11). All other lakes share one colour.\n\nLoading the elevation grid…\n\nWater level\n–\nPercentile\n–\nLakes\n–\nArea flooded\n–\n\nNo lake selected\n\nClick a bar, or use a button in the “Longest-lived lakes” table, to see where a lake is born and where it spills.\n\nPlay\nSpeed\n0.25×\n0.5×\n1×\n2×\n4×\nPacing\nPercentile\nLinear (metres)\nWater level\n–\nMin persistence\n10.0 m\nTop bars\n40 bars\nUnits\nm\nft\nView\nBarcode\nDiagram\nBasin mask\n\nUSGS Watershed Boundary Dataset regions 14–15 (Upper Colorado Region and Lower Colorado Region). Off by default. It hatches the map outside the basin and marks each lake in the table below; the lakes and the barcode are still computed on the whole rectangle.\n\nPlace labels\n\nShow nearby names from GNIS. When labels would overlap, the map gives priority to selected landmarks. See all places and coordinates.\n\nWith the map focused: Space plays or pauses, Left and Right arrows move the water level (with Shift, in bigger steps), Home and End jump to the bottom and top, Up and Down select the previous or next bar, and Escape clears the selection.\n\nLongest-l",
