  "/research/water-quality/": {
    "page": {
      "url": "https://andrewchincea.com/research/water-quality/",
      "title": "🐴 Water-quality prediction on river networks · Andrew Chincea",
      "w": 1440,
      "h": 900,
      "sx": 0,
      "sy": 0,
      "pw": 1440,
      "ph": 1910
    },
    "dom": {
      "result": {
        "type": "object",
        "value": {
          "path": "/research/water-quality/",
          "background": "rgb(17, 19, 21)",
          "hook": true,
          "settings": false,
          "h1": "Water-quality prediction on river networks",
          "noscript": [],
          "content": "RESEARCH\n\nWater-quality prediction on river networks\nIllustration\nAbout\n\nI use graph-Laplacian regression on river networks to predict hourly water quality at stations that have no data.\n\nI work on RiverNets, a project in the TDA Lab at Kennesaw State University. The model predicts five measurements (water temperature, dissolved oxygen, specific conductance, pH and turbidity) with a smoothness penalty along the river network.\n\nPosition\nUndergraduate Research Assistant – RiverNets (TDA Lab), Kennesaw State University · Aug 2026 – Present\nAdvisor\nDr. Mehmet Aktas\n\nThe Rising water explorer uses elevation data for the same Colorado River Basin study area that RiverNets covers.\n\nMethods\nGraph-Laplacian regression with a smoothness penalty along the river network.\nHourly USGS continuous water-quality records from 28 Colorado River Basin stations, cleaned and audited for completeness by station and variable.\nRiver-network graphs built in NetworkX from MERIT Hydro 90 m flow directions (67,401 nodes for the Colorado basin, compressed to 67), plus graphs for the Chattahoochee basin.\nKron (Schur-complement) reduction that collapses the network exactly onto the station nodes, matching the full Laplacian to within 10⁻⁸ while running about 150× faster.\nBenchmarks against OLS, Ridge, Elastic Net, a spline GAM, random forest and gradient boosting under nested leave-one-station-out validation, plus three-station and whole-subbasin holdouts.\nResults\nAt unseen stations the graph model reached R² = 0.38 for specific conductance and 0.12 for pH, versus 0.02 for the best alternative.\nERA5-Land air temperature raised water-temperature R² from 0.863 to 0.892, and a graph–gradient-boosting blend",
          "controls": [
            [
              "Dark theme",
              false
            ]
          ],
          "links": [
            [
              "RESEARCH",
              "/research/"
            ],
            [
              "Rising water explorer",
              "/rising-water/"
            ],
            [
              "Rising water",
              "/rising-water/"
            ],
